(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("iac-beyond",{"iac-beyond/opentofu-and-the-terraform-ecosystem-choices":{"html":"\u003cp>Terraform and OpenTofu share a language and most providers, but they are separate projects with different licences and release paths. Knowing what is the same and what differs lets you choose on purpose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why OpenTofu exists and who governs it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is compatible: HCL, providers and state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What differs: features, registry and licence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A safe migration procedure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning versions and keeping options open\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"background\">Background\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>OpenTofu is an open source fork of Terraform, hosted by the Linux Foundation, started after Terraform moved to a source available licence. It keeps the HCL language and uses the same provider protocol, so most providers and modules work in both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compatibility\">Compatibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a given version range, the state format and configuration are largely compatible, which makes switching practical. Features have diverged over time, so check the release notes of both tools. Test your own modules instead of trusting a general claim.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"differences\">Differences\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Examples are features added in only one project, different default registries and different licence terms for the tool itself. Providers are separate software with their own licences. Check how your vendor, your cloud provider and your compliance team treat each licence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-safe-migration\">A safe migration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Back up the state, pin the current tool version, run a plan with the current tool and save the output. Then run init and plan with the new tool in a copy of the environment and compare. The plan should show no changes. Switch one stack at a time and keep a rollback by restoring the state backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-options-open\">Keep options open\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pin the required version in code, avoid tool specific features in shared modules and run your CI against the versions you support. This reduces the cost of changing later in either direction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"code&gt;tool;tool&gt;prov;tool&gt;state\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">OpenTofu and the Terraform ecosystem choices\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"code\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">HCL code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tool\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Terraform or OpenTofu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prov\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Providers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"state\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, HCL code to Terraform or OpenTofu; 2, Terraform or OpenTofu to Providers; 3, Terraform or OpenTofu to State.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"code\">\u003cdt>HCL code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Same language.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tool specific features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tool\">\u003cdt>Terraform or OpenTofu \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Plans and applies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Version drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prov\">\u003cdt>Providers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Talk to APIs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Version mismatch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"state\">\u003cdt>State \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Corrupted or lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-plan-that-wanted-to-replace-a-database\">Incident: A plan that wanted to replace a database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After switching tools, the first plan showed a destroy and create of a production database.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tofu plan -no-color | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>tofu plan -no-color | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-/+ resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;main&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The provider version was not pinned, so the new tool installed a newer provider that changed a default. The team stopped before applying, pinned the provider to the previous version, saw a clean plan and scheduled the provider upgrade as its own change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Switching tools and upgrading providers in one step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not pinning provider and tool versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying a plan nobody read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming every feature exists in both tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No state backup before a migration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"background","text":"Background"},{"id":"compatibility","text":"Compatibility"},{"id":"differences","text":"Differences"},{"id":"a-safe-migration","text":"A safe migration"},{"id":"keep-options-open","text":"Keep options open"},{"id":"incident-a-plan-that-wanted-to-replace-a-database","text":"Incident: A plan that wanted to replace a database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenTofu documentation","u":"https://opentofu.org/docs/"},{"t":"OpenTofu repository","u":"https://github.com/opentofu/opentofu"},{"t":"Terraform state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"}],"text":"Terraform and OpenTofu share a language and most providers, but they are separate projects with different licences and release paths. Knowing what is the same and what differs lets you choose on purpose. What you will learn Why OpenTofu exists and who governs it What is compatible: HCL, providers and state What differs: features, registry and licence A safe migration procedure Pinning versions and keeping options open Background OpenTofu is an open source fork of Terraform, hosted by the Linux Foundation, started after Terraform moved to a source available licence. It keeps the HCL language and uses the same provider protocol, so most providers and modules work in both. Compatibility For a given version range, the state format and configuration are largely compatible, which makes switching practical. Features have diverged over time, so check the release notes of both tools. Test your own modules instead of trusting a general claim. Differences Examples are features added in only one project, different default registries and different licence terms for the tool itself. Providers are separate software with their own licences. Check how your vendor, your cloud provider and your compliance team treat each licence. A safe migration Back up the state, pin the current tool version, run a plan with the current tool and save the output. Then run init and plan with the new tool in a copy of the environment and compare. The plan should show no changes. Switch one stack at a time and keep a rollback by restoring the state backup. Keep options open Pin the required version in code, avoid tool specific features in shared modules and run your CI against the versions you support. This reduces the cost of changing later in either direction. OpenTofu and the Terraform ecosystem choices Flow in order: 1, HCL code to Terraform or OpenTofu; 2, Terraform or OpenTofu to Providers; 3, Terraform or OpenTofu to State. Outside party Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. HCL code Outside party What it does: Same language. If it fails or is weak: Tool specific features. Terraform or OpenTofu Step What it does: Plans and applies. If it fails or is weak: Version drift. Providers Service What it does: Talk to APIs. If it fails or is weak: Version mismatch. State Data store What it does: Records resources. If it fails or is weak: Corrupted or lost. Incident: A plan that wanted to replace a database Incident After switching tools, the first plan showed a destroy and create of a production database. $ tofu plan -no-color | tail -4 # aws_db_instance.main must be replaced -/+ resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"main\" { Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy. (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The provider version was not pinned, so the new tool installed a newer provider that changed a default. The team stopped before applying, pinned the provider to the previous version, saw a clean plan and scheduled the provider upgrade as its own change. Common mistakes Switching tools and upgrading providers in one step Not pinning provider and tool versions Applying a plan nobody read Assuming every feature exists in both tools No state backup before a migration"},"iac-beyond/pulumi-infrastructure-in-real-programming-languages":{"html":"\u003cp>Pulumi uses general purpose languages to declare resources. You get loops, functions, types and tests, and you also get more ways to write unreadable infrastructure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Pulumi model: programs, stacks and state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing resources in Python\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inputs, outputs and apply\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets and configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When code helps and when HCL is simpler\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"model\">Model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Pulumi program declares resources and the engine compares them with the state of a stack, then creates, updates or deletes what differs. A stack is one instance of the program, such as dev or prod, with its own configuration and state. State can live in the Pulumi service or in your own object storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resources-in-code\">Resources in code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A resource is an object created from a provider class. Because it is a normal program, you can use loops for many similar resources, functions for shared patterns and types to catch mistakes before a run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inputs-and-outputs\">Inputs and outputs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some values are known only after a resource exists, so Pulumi wraps them as outputs. Use \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> to transform an output, and pass outputs directly as inputs of other resources so the engine sees the dependency. Printing an output as a string before it resolves shows a placeholder, not the value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-and-configuration\">Secrets and configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Mark values as secrets with \u003ccode>pulumi config set --secret\u003c/code> and the state stores them encrypted. Configuration is per stack, which keeps differences between environments in one place instead of copied files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"code-or-configuration\">Code or configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Code shines for repeated patterns, validation and tests with ordinary test frameworks. It is a poor fit when the team lacks the language skills, or when plain declarative files would be clearer. Keep programs small, avoid network calls in code and review them like application code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prog&gt;eng;eng&gt;state;eng&gt;cloud\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Pulumi: infrastructure in real programming languages\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prog\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Program\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eng\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pulumi engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"state\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stack state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cloud\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Program to Pulumi engine; 2, Pulumi engine to Stack state; 3, Pulumi engine to Cloud API.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prog\">\u003cdt>Program \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Declares resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hidden logic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eng\">\u003cdt>Pulumi engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Diffs against state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong stack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"state\">\u003cdt>Stack state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypted secrets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost backend.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cloud\">\u003cdt>Cloud API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Permissions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-output-used-before-it-existed\">Incident: An output used before it existed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script printed a bucket name that never worked in a connection string.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pulumi stack output bucketUrl\npulumi preview --diff | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pulumi stack output bucketUrl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">s3://app-assets-1f3a9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pulumi preview --diff | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">~ aws:s3/bucket:Bucket: (update)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    [id=app-assets-1f3a9]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The program built the URL by joining plain strings with the output object, which produced an unresolved placeholder in the application. Using apply to build the string after the value existed fixed it, and a unit test now checks the final value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating outputs as plain strings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One huge program for every environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in plain configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network calls and randomness inside programs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No preview before an update\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"model","text":"Model"},{"id":"resources-in-code","text":"Resources in code"},{"id":"inputs-and-outputs","text":"Inputs and outputs"},{"id":"secrets-and-configuration","text":"Secrets and configuration"},{"id":"code-or-configuration","text":"Code or configuration"},{"id":"incident-an-output-used-before-it-existed","text":"Incident: An output used before it existed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pulumi","u":"https://github.com/pulumi/pulumi"},{"t":"OpenTofu repository","u":"https://github.com/opentofu/opentofu"},{"t":"Terraform state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"}],"text":"Pulumi uses general purpose languages to declare resources. You get loops, functions, types and tests, and you also get more ways to write unreadable infrastructure. What you will learn The Pulumi model: programs, stacks and state Writing resources in Python Inputs, outputs and apply Secrets and configuration When code helps and when HCL is simpler Model A Pulumi program declares resources and the engine compares them with the state of a stack, then creates, updates or deletes what differs. A stack is one instance of the program, such as dev or prod, with its own configuration and state. State can live in the Pulumi service or in your own object storage. Resources in code A resource is an object created from a provider class. Because it is a normal program, you can use loops for many similar resources, functions for shared patterns and types to catch mistakes before a run. Inputs and outputs Some values are known only after a resource exists, so Pulumi wraps them as outputs. Use apply to transform an output, and pass outputs directly as inputs of other resources so the engine sees the dependency. Printing an output as a string before it resolves shows a placeholder, not the value. Secrets and configuration Mark values as secrets with pulumi config set --secret and the state stores them encrypted. Configuration is per stack, which keeps differences between environments in one place instead of copied files. Code or configuration Code shines for repeated patterns, validation and tests with ordinary test frameworks. It is a poor fit when the team lacks the language skills, or when plain declarative files would be clearer. Keep programs small, avoid network calls in code and review them like application code. Pulumi: infrastructure in real programming languages Flow in order: 1, Program to Pulumi engine; 2, Pulumi engine to Stack state; 3, Pulumi engine to Cloud API. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Program Outside party What it does: Declares resources. If it fails or is weak: Hidden logic. Pulumi engine Step What it does: Diffs against state. If it fails or is weak: Wrong stack. Stack state Data store What it does: Encrypted secrets. If it fails or is weak: Lost backend. Cloud API Service What it does: Creates resources. If it fails or is weak: Permissions. Incident: An output used before it existed Incident A script printed a bucket name that never worked in a connection string. $ pulumi stack output bucketUrl s3://app-assets-1f3a9 $ pulumi preview --diff | head -4 ~ aws:s3/bucket:Bucket: (update) [id=app-assets-1f3a9] (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The program built the URL by joining plain strings with the output object, which produced an unresolved placeholder in the application. Using apply to build the string after the value existed fixed it, and a unit test now checks the final value. Common mistakes Treating outputs as plain strings One huge program for every environment Secrets in plain configuration Network calls and randomness inside programs No preview before an update"},"iac-beyond/terragrunt-modules-and-multi-environment-structure":{"html":"\u003cp>Copying a Terraform folder for each environment works until the fifth copy. Good structure separates reusable modules from environment configuration and limits the size of each state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Modules versus environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Small states and blast radius\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What Terragrunt adds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependencies between stacks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning modules and promoting changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modules-and-environments\">Modules and environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A module is a reusable unit such as a network or a database. An environment folder calls modules with its own values. Modules live in a separate repository or directory and are versioned, so that production can use version 1.4 while dev tests 1.5.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius\">Blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One huge state means every plan reads everything and a mistake can touch everything. Split by lifecycle and risk: network, data, compute and applications each have their own state. A failure or a lock affects only that part.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terragrunt\">Terragrunt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terragrunt is a thin wrapper that generates backend and provider configuration, passes inputs from a hierarchy of files and runs several stacks in order. It removes repeated backend blocks and keeps the folder tree consistent. It also adds a layer that people must learn, so use it when repetition is a real problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dependencies\">Dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A stack often needs outputs from another, such as the network id. Terragrunt dependency blocks read those outputs and define an order. Mock outputs let plans run before the dependency exists, but they must never be used in an apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"promotion\">Promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Change a module in a branch, release a tag, then update the version in dev, then staging and then production, reading the plan each time. A short, boring pipeline with a manual approval for production is better than a fast pipeline with surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"mod&gt;env;env&gt;tg;tg&gt;st\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Terragrunt, modules and multi-environment structure\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mod\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Module repo\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"env\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Environment folders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Terragrunt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"st\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Separate states\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Module repo to Environment folders; 2, Environment folders to Terragrunt; 3, Terragrunt to Separate states.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mod\">\u003cdt>Module repo \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Versioned modules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpinned refs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"env\">\u003cdt>Environment folders \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inputs per environment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Copy and paste.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tg\">\u003cdt>Terragrunt \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Backends and order.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hidden magic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"st\">\u003cdt>Separate states \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Small blast radius.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One big state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-single-apply-that-touched-everything\">Incident: A single apply that touched everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change to a tag in a shared network stack planned changes for 400 resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terragrunt run-all plan --terragrunt-non-interactive 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -c &quot;will be updated in-place&quot;\ngit diff --stat | tail -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terragrunt run-all plan --terragrunt-non-interactive 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;will be updated in-place&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">412\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git diff --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All environments pointed to the unversioned main branch of the module, so a one line change affected every stack at once. The team pinned module versions by tag, promoted them through dev and staging and split the network stack so a change touches fewer resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pointing modules at a branch instead of a tag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One state for the whole company\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mock outputs used in an apply\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying folders for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review of the plan for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"modules-and-environments","text":"Modules and environments"},{"id":"blast-radius","text":"Blast radius"},{"id":"terragrunt","text":"Terragrunt"},{"id":"dependencies","text":"Dependencies"},{"id":"promotion","text":"Promotion"},{"id":"incident-a-single-apply-that-touched-everything","text":"Incident: A single apply that touched everything"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terragrunt","u":"https://github.com/gruntwork-io/terragrunt"},{"t":"Terraform state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"},{"t":"OpenTofu documentation","u":"https://opentofu.org/docs/"}],"text":"Copying a Terraform folder for each environment works until the fifth copy. Good structure separates reusable modules from environment configuration and limits the size of each state. What you will learn Modules versus environments Small states and blast radius What Terragrunt adds Dependencies between stacks Versioning modules and promoting changes Modules and environments A module is a reusable unit such as a network or a database. An environment folder calls modules with its own values. Modules live in a separate repository or directory and are versioned, so that production can use version 1.4 while dev tests 1.5. Blast radius One huge state means every plan reads everything and a mistake can touch everything. Split by lifecycle and risk: network, data, compute and applications each have their own state. A failure or a lock affects only that part. Terragrunt Terragrunt is a thin wrapper that generates backend and provider configuration, passes inputs from a hierarchy of files and runs several stacks in order. It removes repeated backend blocks and keeps the folder tree consistent. It also adds a layer that people must learn, so use it when repetition is a real problem. Dependencies A stack often needs outputs from another, such as the network id. Terragrunt dependency blocks read those outputs and define an order. Mock outputs let plans run before the dependency exists, but they must never be used in an apply. Promotion Change a module in a branch, release a tag, then update the version in dev, then staging and then production, reading the plan each time. A short, boring pipeline with a manual approval for production is better than a fast pipeline with surprises. Terragrunt, modules and multi-environment structure Flow in order: 1, Module repo to Environment folders; 2, Environment folders to Terragrunt; 3, Terragrunt to Separate states. Outside party Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Module repo Outside party What it does: Versioned modules. If it fails or is weak: Unpinned refs. Environment folders Step What it does: Inputs per environment. If it fails or is weak: Copy and paste. Terragrunt Step What it does: Backends and order. If it fails or is weak: Hidden magic. Separate states Data store What it does: Small blast radius. If it fails or is weak: One big state. Incident: A single apply that touched everything Incident A change to a tag in a shared network stack planned changes for 400 resources. $ terragrunt run-all plan --terragrunt-non-interactive 2>&1 | grep -c \"will be updated in-place\" 412 $ git diff -- stat | tail -1 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) All environments pointed to the unversioned main branch of the module, so a one line change affected every stack at once. The team pinned module versions by tag, promoted them through dev and staging and split the network stack so a change touches fewer resources. Common mistakes Pointing modules at a branch instead of a tag One state for the whole company Mock outputs used in an apply Copying folders for each environment No review of the plan for each environment"},"iac-beyond/testing-infrastructure-code":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure changes are expensive to get wrong, yet often untested. A layered approach catches simple mistakes in seconds and reserves slow real deployments for what only they can prove.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The testing pyramid for infrastructure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Format, validate and lint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Static security checks and policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Native tests and plan assertions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Integration tests with real resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pyramid\">The pyramid\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cheap fast checks run on every commit, medium checks run on every pull request and expensive tests run on a schedule or before a release. Spend most effort at the cheap end and add expensive tests for modules that many teams use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format-validate-lint\">Format, validate, lint\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>fmt -check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>validate\u003c/code> and a linter such as tflint to catch syntax errors, unused variables and invalid instance types. They need no credentials and finish in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-security-checks\">Static security checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scan plans or code with tools such as Checkov, Trivy or a policy engine to find open security groups, unencrypted storage and public buckets before anything is created. Treat findings as review comments with clear fixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-tests\">Native tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terraform and OpenTofu support test files with run blocks and assertions. A test can use a plan only to check expected values, or apply into a temporary environment and destroy afterward. Test module inputs and outputs, and the validation rules you wrote.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"integration-tests\">Integration tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tools such as Terratest deploy real resources, check them and destroy them. They give strong confidence but take minutes and cost money. Run them for shared modules, use a separate account with budget limits and make sure cleanup runs even when a test fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"fast&gt;sec;sec&gt;nat;nat&gt;int\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Testing infrastructure code\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fast\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Format and validate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Static checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan and native tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"int\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Real deployment tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Format and validate to Static checks; 2, Static checks to Plan and native tests; 3, Plan and native tests to Real deployment tests.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fast\">\u003cdt>Format and validate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped locally.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Static checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Security rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored findings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nat\">\u003cdt>Plan and native tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Assertions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Weak assertions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"int\">\u003cdt>Real deployment tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Real resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Orphaned resources.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-test-environment-left-running-for-a-month\">Incident: A test environment left running for a month\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An integration test failed midway and left a database and a load balancer running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --tag-filters Key=env,Values=test --query 'ResourceTagMappingList[].ResourceARN' --output text | wc -w\naws ce get-cost-and-usage --help | head -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --tag-filters Key=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span>,Values=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ResourceTagMappingList[].ResourceARN&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -w\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-cost-and-usage --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">help\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GET-COST-AND-USAGE()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The test destroyed resources only on success. Cleanup now runs in a final step that always executes, tests tag everything with an owner and expiry, and a nightly job deletes anything past its expiry in the test account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running only manual plans\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Integration tests with no cleanup guarantee\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that assert nothing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Findings from scanners ignored for months\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing in the production account\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pyramid","text":"The pyramid"},{"id":"format-validate-lint","text":"Format, validate, lint"},{"id":"static-security-checks","text":"Static security checks"},{"id":"native-tests","text":"Native tests"},{"id":"integration-tests","text":"Integration tests"},{"id":"incident-a-test-environment-left-running-for-a-month","text":"Incident: A test environment left running for a month"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform tests","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/tests"},{"t":"Terratest","u":"https://github.com/gruntwork-io/terratest"},{"t":"Checkov","u":"https://github.com/bridgecrewio/checkov"}],"text":"Infrastructure changes are expensive to get wrong, yet often untested. A layered approach catches simple mistakes in seconds and reserves slow real deployments for what only they can prove. What you will learn The testing pyramid for infrastructure Format, validate and lint Static security checks and policy Native tests and plan assertions Integration tests with real resources The pyramid Cheap fast checks run on every commit, medium checks run on every pull request and expensive tests run on a schedule or before a release. Spend most effort at the cheap end and add expensive tests for modules that many teams use. Format, validate, lint Use fmt -check , validate and a linter such as tflint to catch syntax errors, unused variables and invalid instance types. They need no credentials and finish in seconds. Static security checks Scan plans or code with tools such as Checkov, Trivy or a policy engine to find open security groups, unencrypted storage and public buckets before anything is created. Treat findings as review comments with clear fixes. Native tests Terraform and OpenTofu support test files with run blocks and assertions. A test can use a plan only to check expected values, or apply into a temporary environment and destroy afterward. Test module inputs and outputs, and the validation rules you wrote. Integration tests Tools such as Terratest deploy real resources, check them and destroy them. They give strong confidence but take minutes and cost money. Run them for shared modules, use a separate account with budget limits and make sure cleanup runs even when a test fails. Testing infrastructure code Flow in order: 1, Format and validate to Static checks; 2, Static checks to Plan and native tests; 3, Plan and native tests to Real deployment tests. Step Security Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Format and validate Step What it does: Seconds. If it fails or is weak: Skipped locally. Static checks Security What it does: Security rules. If it fails or is weak: Ignored findings. Plan and native tests Step What it does: Assertions. If it fails or is weak: Weak assertions. Real deployment tests Service What it does: Real resources. If it fails or is weak: Orphaned resources. Incident: A test environment left running for a month Incident An integration test failed midway and left a database and a load balancer running. $ aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --tag-filters Key= env ,Values= test --query &#x27;ResourceTagMappingList[].ResourceARN&#x27; --output text | wc -w 14 $ aws ce get-cost-and-usage -- help | head -1 GET-COST-AND-USAGE() (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The test destroyed resources only on success. Cleanup now runs in a final step that always executes, tests tag everything with an owner and expiry, and a nightly job deletes anything past its expiry in the test account. Common mistakes Running only manual plans Integration tests with no cleanup guarantee Tests that assert nothing Findings from scanners ignored for months Testing in the production account"},"iac-beyond/policy-as-code-and-drift-detection":{"html":"\u003cp>Reviews miss things. Policy as code applies the same rules to every plan, and drift detection finds the changes people made by hand so the code and reality match again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to enforce with policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policies against plan output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where to run policy in the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detecting and classifying drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reverting or adopting drift\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-enforce\">What to enforce\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Good policies are specific and explainable: storage must be encrypted, only approved regions and instance sizes are allowed, public access needs an approval tag, every resource has an owner tag and cost centre. Avoid broad rules that no one can satisfy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policies-on-a-plan\">Policies on a plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A plan can be exported as JSON, and a policy engine such as Open Policy Agent with Conftest evaluates rules against it. Each rule returns a message that says what is wrong and how to fix it. Write tests for the policies themselves, with examples that pass and fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"in-the-pipeline\">In the pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run policy on every pull request so the author sees failures early, and run it again before apply. Warnings for new rules and hard failures for established rules let teams adapt. An exemption process with an owner and an expiry avoids silent bypasses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detecting-drift\">Detecting drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Drift happens when someone changes a resource in the console or another tool. A scheduled plan, with no changes expected, reports differences. Classify drift: a harmless tag change, a risky security group change or an emergency fix that should become code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revert-or-adopt\">Revert or adopt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the change was wrong, apply the code to revert it and find out why access allowed it. If it was a valid emergency fix, copy it into code through a pull request so the next apply does not undo it. Reduce console write access to cut drift at the source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;pol;pol&gt;apply;apply&gt;drift\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Policy as code and drift detection\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan JSON\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apply\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drift\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drift check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Plan JSON to Policy engine; 2, Policy engine to Apply; 3, Apply to Drift check.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Plan JSON \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Proposed changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreviewed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Evaluates rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Weak rules.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apply\">\u003cdt>Apply \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bypass.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drift\">\u003cdt>Drift check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scheduled plan.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored report.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-security-group-opened-in-the-console\">Incident: A security group opened in the console\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an incident someone opened a port to the internet and never closed it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color | tail -5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_security_group.web will be updated in-place\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  ~ ingress {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      + cidr_blocks = [&quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The nightly drift plan reported the change and a ticket was opened automatically. The team reverted the rule through code, reviewed who could edit security groups in the console and added a policy that denies public ingress without an approved tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Policies nobody can explain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policies with no tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exemptions without owner or expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never running a plan against reality\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing drift by hand a second time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-enforce","text":"What to enforce"},{"id":"policies-on-a-plan","text":"Policies on a plan"},{"id":"in-the-pipeline","text":"In the pipeline"},{"id":"detecting-drift","text":"Detecting drift"},{"id":"revert-or-adopt","text":"Revert or adopt"},{"id":"incident-a-security-group-opened-in-the-console","text":"Incident: A security group opened in the console"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Open Policy Agent","u":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/"},{"t":"Checkov","u":"https://github.com/bridgecrewio/checkov"},{"t":"Terraform state","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state"}],"text":"Reviews miss things. Policy as code applies the same rules to every plan, and drift detection finds the changes people made by hand so the code and reality match again. What you will learn What to enforce with policy Policies against plan output Where to run policy in the pipeline Detecting and classifying drift Reverting or adopting drift What to enforce Good policies are specific and explainable: storage must be encrypted, only approved regions and instance sizes are allowed, public access needs an approval tag, every resource has an owner tag and cost centre. Avoid broad rules that no one can satisfy. Policies on a plan A plan can be exported as JSON, and a policy engine such as Open Policy Agent with Conftest evaluates rules against it. Each rule returns a message that says what is wrong and how to fix it. Write tests for the policies themselves, with examples that pass and fail. In the pipeline Run policy on every pull request so the author sees failures early, and run it again before apply. Warnings for new rules and hard failures for established rules let teams adapt. An exemption process with an owner and an expiry avoids silent bypasses. Detecting drift Drift happens when someone changes a resource in the console or another tool. A scheduled plan, with no changes expected, reports differences. Classify drift: a harmless tag change, a risky security group change or an emergency fix that should become code. Revert or adopt If the change was wrong, apply the code to revert it and find out why access allowed it. If it was a valid emergency fix, copy it into code through a pull request so the next apply does not undo it. Reduce console write access to cut drift at the source. Policy as code and drift detection Flow in order: 1, Plan JSON to Policy engine; 2, Policy engine to Apply; 3, Apply to Drift check. Step Security Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Plan JSON Step What it does: Proposed changes. If it fails or is weak: Unreviewed. Policy engine Security What it does: Evaluates rules. If it fails or is weak: Weak rules. Apply Service What it does: Creates changes. If it fails or is weak: Bypass. Drift check Observability What it does: Scheduled plan. If it fails or is weak: Ignored report. Incident: A security group opened in the console Incident During an incident someone opened a port to the internet and never closed it. $ terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color | tail -5 # aws_security_group.web will be updated in-place ~ ingress { + cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"] Plan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy. (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The nightly drift plan reported the change and a ticket was opened automatically. The team reverted the rule through code, reviewed who could edit security groups in the console and added a policy that denies public ingress without an approved tag. Common mistakes Policies nobody can explain Policies with no tests Exemptions without owner or expiry Never running a plan against reality Fixing drift by hand a second time"},"iac-beyond/state-secrets-and-safe-migrations":{"html":"\u003cp>State is the memory of your infrastructure tool, and it often contains secrets. Refactoring code without telling the tool what moved leads to destroy and create. Safe procedures avoid both problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Remote state, locking and access control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in state and in code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving resources with moved blocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Importing existing resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing from state without destroying\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"remote-state\">Remote state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store state in a remote backend such as an object store with versioning, encryption and a lock table, so only one apply runs at a time and you can roll back a bad write. Limit read access, because state may contain passwords and keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not put secrets in code or variable files in the repository. Read them at run time from a secrets manager, mark outputs as sensitive and be aware that sensitive values are still stored in state. Prefer resources that generate and store secrets in the manager without exposing them to the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"moved-blocks\">Moved blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you rename a resource or move it into a module, the tool sees a delete and a create. A \u003ccode>moved\u003c/code> block tells it the old address and the new address so the real resource is kept. Review the plan: it should show moves and no replacements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"import\">Import\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To bring an existing resource under management, write the resource block and use an \u003ccode>import\u003c/code> block or the import command with its id. Run a plan until it shows no changes, adjusting the code to match reality. Import in small steps and keep a backup of state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"removing-safely\">Removing safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To stop managing something without deleting it, use a \u003ccode>removed\u003c/code> block with \u003ccode>lifecycle { destroy = false }\u003c/code> or the state remove command. Use \u003ccode>prevent_destroy\u003c/code> on critical resources such as databases so a mistaken plan fails loudly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"code&gt;mv;mv&gt;lock;lock&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">State, secrets and safe migrations\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"code\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Refactored code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mv\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Moved and import blocks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lock\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Remote backend and lock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Real resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Refactored code to Moved and import blocks; 2, Moved and import blocks to Remote backend and lock; 3, Remote backend and lock to Real resources.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"code\">\u003cdt>Refactored code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> New addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Renames.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mv\">\u003cdt>Moved and import blocks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Map old to new.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing blocks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lock\">\u003cdt>Remote backend and lock \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One apply at a time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stuck lock.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Real resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Kept intact.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Recreated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-rename-that-planned-to-delete-the-database\">Incident: A rename that planned to delete the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A refactor into modules changed resource addresses and the plan showed a destroy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan -no-color | grep -E &quot;destroyed|created&quot; | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan -no-color | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;destroyed|created&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # aws_db_instance.main will be destroyed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  # module.data.aws_db_instance.main will be created\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The code moved the resource into a module without a moved block. The team added a moved block from the old to the new address, the next plan showed only a move, and prevent_destroy was added to the database as a guard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Renaming resources without moved blocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>State in a local file or a repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in tfvars files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying with no lock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No prevent_destroy on databases\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"remote-state","text":"Remote state"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"moved-blocks","text":"Moved blocks"},{"id":"import","text":"Import"},{"id":"removing-safely","text":"Removing safely"},{"id":"incident-a-rename-that-planned-to-delete-the-database","text":"Incident: A rename that planned to delete the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Importing resources","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/import"},{"t":"Refactoring modules","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/refactoring"},{"t":"Managing sensitive data","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/manage-sensitive-data"}],"text":"State is the memory of your infrastructure tool, and it often contains secrets. Refactoring code without telling the tool what moved leads to destroy and create. Safe procedures avoid both problems. What you will learn Remote state, locking and access control Secrets in state and in code Moving resources with moved blocks Importing existing resources Removing from state without destroying Remote state Store state in a remote backend such as an object store with versioning, encryption and a lock table, so only one apply runs at a time and you can roll back a bad write. Limit read access, because state may contain passwords and keys. Secrets Do not put secrets in code or variable files in the repository. Read them at run time from a secrets manager, mark outputs as sensitive and be aware that sensitive values are still stored in state. Prefer resources that generate and store secrets in the manager without exposing them to the tool. Moved blocks When you rename a resource or move it into a module, the tool sees a delete and a create. A moved block tells it the old address and the new address so the real resource is kept. Review the plan: it should show moves and no replacements. Import To bring an existing resource under management, write the resource block and use an import block or the import command with its id. Run a plan until it shows no changes, adjusting the code to match reality. Import in small steps and keep a backup of state. Removing safely To stop managing something without deleting it, use a removed block with lifecycle { destroy = false } or the state remove command. Use prevent_destroy on critical resources such as databases so a mistaken plan fails loudly. State, secrets and safe migrations Flow in order: 1, Refactored code to Moved and import blocks; 2, Moved and import blocks to Remote backend and lock; 3, Remote backend and lock to Real resources. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Refactored code Outside party What it does: New addresses. If it fails or is weak: Renames. Moved and import blocks Step What it does: Map old to new. If it fails or is weak: Missing blocks. Remote backend and lock Data store What it does: One apply at a time. If it fails or is weak: Stuck lock. Real resources Service What it does: Kept intact. If it fails or is weak: Recreated. Incident: A rename that planned to delete the database Incident A refactor into modules changed resource addresses and the plan showed a destroy. $ terraform plan -no-color | grep -E \"destroyed|created\" | head -3 # aws_db_instance.main will be destroyed # module.data.aws_db_instance.main will be created Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy. (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The code moved the resource into a module without a moved block. The team added a moved block from the old to the new address, the next plan showed only a move, and prevent_destroy was added to the database as a guard. Common mistakes Renaming resources without moved blocks State in a local file or a repository Secrets in tfvars files Applying with no lock No prevent_destroy on databases"}});